#+TITLE: AutoCAD bootstrap timeout leaves a detached COM engine and blocks subsequent CI builds
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: compatibility
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-10-29

* Confirmed failure sequence

[[https://fabrik.sncf.fr/gitlab/dmoe-pop/epure/es/schms/-/jobs/558988][SCHMS EPURE AutoCAD job 558988]] ran on runner2 from 12:14:08 to 12:17:12
Europe/Paris on 2026-10-29. Alfe reported READY-TIMEOUT after 120 seconds,
last state BOOTING, and terminated its spawned engine PID 22244.

A separate =acad.exe /Automation -Embedding= process, PID 18312, survived.
Its parent was the RPC service PID 952, not PID 22244. CIM confirmed owner
runner2, session 18, creation 11:14:54.448 UTC, inside the failed job.
Read-only process-handle inspection tied it unambiguously to
=travail/runner2/558988/temp/= through two live temporary files. No foreign
project path was used as ownership evidence.

The survivor prevented later jobs from compiling because the legitimate
preflight guard refuses to compile while AutoCAD is open in the same session:

- [[https://fabrik.sncf.fr/gitlab/dmoe-pop/epure/es/schms/-/jobs/559025][compile job 559025]], another branch, failed before compilation;
- jobs 559119 (optimized DES qualification) and 559122 (compile) also failed
  at this guard. This is not evidence of a DES or product Lisp regression.

After confirming that job 558988 had ended, the survivor was closed using
its exact PID, creation timestamp, owner, session and job-temporary-handle
evidence. Only its process tree was terminated; the RPC parent and unrelated
CAD instances were preserved. A follow-up CIM query confirmed PID 18312 gone.
The first immediate CIM query after taskkill still returned the terminated
process: cleanup must wait for the actual postcondition, not only request it.

* Cause to fix independently of the original bootstrap failure

Cleanup tracked the spawned launcher/engine but missed the different CAD
server activated through COM. Killing only the launcher's process tree cannot
reach a server whose parent is the RPC service. Bootstrap failure must not
leave an owned detached server behind even when READY was never reached.

The trace also warned that TRUSTEDPATHS could not be extended in the EPURE
profile. Subsequent read-only inspection found both runner profiles present,
owner FullControl, ExpandString values and SECURELOAD already 0. No registry
value or ACL was changed. The warning alone therefore does not establish the
reason for BOOTING; do not conflate this cleanup bug with that separate cause.
The earlier session-state investigation remains in
[[file:autocad-com-fails-in-disconnected-windows-session.issue][autocad-com-fails-in-disconnected-windows-session.issue]].

* Acceptance and regression test

Create a deterministic bootstrap-failure fixture for a fresh AutoCAD COM
server, including the case where its PID differs from the spawned process
and is parented by RPC. Fail before READY and verify that all processes
positively owned by that invocation terminate within a bounded delay.
Also cover cancellation, early bridge failure and normal shutdown.

Keep a pre-existing CAD/COM process and a foreign job alive during the test;
prove they remain untouched. A candidate created in the same account/session
is not sufficient ownership evidence. Use the invocation's COM reference or
an equally strong per-invocation marker (for this incident, the private job
temp handles supplied that evidence). Never kill all acad.exe processes or
the RPC service. Retain process receipts and bootstrap logs on failure, then
replay EPURE AutoCAD and the following compilation in the same runner session.

* Recurrence with ALFE 2.3.78 -- 2026-10-30

[[https://fabrik.sncf.fr/gitlab/dmoe-pop/epure/es/schms/-/jobs/561306][SCHMS ActiveX AutoCAD job 561306]], pipeline 91776, ran from 11:09:33 to
11:12:51 Europe/Paris. It reported READY-TIMEOUT, BOOTING, and terminated
spawned launcher PID 7212. No assertion ran.

The archived bridge =com-flags.txt= positively identifies the detached server:

#+begin_example
CREATEDPID=12728
ATTACHED=0
CREATED=1
#+end_example

CIM subsequently confirmed acad.exe PID 12728, owner runner0, session 4,
created 11:10:41 Europe/Paris, RPC parent 952, command line
=acad.exe /Automation -Embedding=. The process survived the completed job.
There was no =owned-cad-process.json= in the uploaded workdir, although
=owned-cad-process.ps1= was emitted. The bridge's early CREATEDPID receipt
therefore exists before the later identity-file capture succeeds.

The server held the checkout-root drawing log =Dessin1_15c15bb02.log=.
Subsequent SCHMS pipeline 91765 jobs 561170 through 561174 failed during
pre_get_sources cleanup, before running their tests. This was a runner0
residue affecting the shared pool, not a runner2 assertion failure.
ALFE 2.3.78 was explicitly verified in the failing job and installed runner.
Updating to that version alone does not close this bootstrap cleanup issue.
The separate normal EPURE AutoCAD job 561184 passed its 3 assertions and
exited successfully under the same installed version.

After checking the completed-job status and the exact CREATEDPID, owner,
session and creation timestamp, only PID 12728 and its descendants were
terminated. The RPC service was preserved. The original drawing log and an
ownership receipt were archived outside the checkout. PID disappearance and
an exclusive FileShare.None open of the released log both succeeded before
moving it out of the checkout. Marker: OWNED_12728_CLOSED_AND_LOG_RELEASED.

Extend the failure-path regression to this gap: COM CreateObject has returned
and CREATEDPID is recorded, but bootstrap never reaches READY and the later
owned-process identity file is absent. Cleanup must use a positively owned
reference or an identity captured before the blocking call; it must still
preserve attached or foreign CAD processes. Requalify this path natively;
normal shutdown coverage alone does not establish it.
